From: pavel@PARK.BU.EDU (Paul Cisek)
Date: Wed, 3 Aug 1994 11:11:39 -0400
Subject: [#94-8-25] Information-theoretic death

>From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
>X-Message-Number: #94-8-18
>
>Tim's assertion that memory of physical skills lies outside the
>brain brings up a very important issue for cryonicists.   Is storing 
>the brain sufficient for avoiding information-theoretic death?

It's very likely that a person revived into a new body will be quite
unable to use it.  The nervous system learns to use the body through a
long process involving circular reactions, trial and error, etc.  Also,
there's a great deal of self-calibration going on all the time while the
body grows and changes.  Thus, a sudden change of the body's mechanical
properties is likely to be a shock.  The newly revived person will not
be able to control his or her limbs or interpret sensory information.

Fortunately, the very same mechanisms of self-calibration that occur
during childhood may be used after revival to retune the system to the
new body.  This may be invoked as an automatic attempt to interpret
inconsistent input, or may require pharmacological intervention, perhaps
involving acetylcholine injections.  The brain is incredibly good at this
kind of self-tuning, as shown by cases of stroke recovery.  The system
may even be able to tune itself to accept a radically different body, with
extra limbs or sensors, for example.  This has been shown in developing
frogs which spontaneously reorganized their visual system to make use of
an implanted third eye (frogs are, of course, very plastic...).

>Emotional memories, which are a big part of one's "identity"
>and may be partially stored in the hormonal system outside the
>brain, are a major unresolved issue.
>
>In general, how much distortion (information loss, including
>loss of all redundancy for that piece of information) in which
>"subsystems" can we accept before we are "information theoretically
>dead"?

This will be a tough judgement to make.  If we insist on drawing a
discrete line between "successful revival" and "death" then any criterion we
choose is likely to be misleading.  We can be reasonably sure that if
the person wakes up and is able to do anything then something has been
revived.  We can also be sure that this person will never be the same as
before.  The question of degrees will be difficult, and the person making
the decision to enter cryogenics will have to make the judgement of how
successful the endeavor is.

>Can we so easily define separate "subsystems" such as 
>intellectual, physical, and emotional, or are these irrevocably 
>intertwined?

I'd say they're irrevocably intertwined.  Divisions along functional
lines can't be taken too deeply into neural function...

Paul Cisek
pavel@cns.bu.edu


