Probably by looking for evidence of selective dendrite growth in
peripheral nerve bundles during learning. Probably been done. Or you
could remove the brain and measure how much skill is retained
... hell, knowing neurophysiologists, that's probably been done, too!

> Well, people with laryngectomies don't complain that they can't think of
> the words -- and when they get their artificial larynx they have no trouble
> using them.
> 
> But do any of you know any paraplegics?  How clearly can they imagine their
> old favorite motor skills?

Two apposite and opposite anecdotals: people who train for a physical
skill, say tennis, often speak of "muscle memory" and I *guess* that
this refers to the neuromuscular "grooving" of particular motions. The
part of this that is just muscular development, including whatever
tuning the peripheral nerves do, would get lost in a rehosting. The
other is the mental training regimens that some athletes do: intensely
visualising the activity, and then doing it. Many swear that practice
time is much better used this way. I used to do this for chess :-)

Everything is so throroughly connected, though, that it's hard to
imagine any of this "muscle memory" stuff not being reflected in the
brain. (I need a good Jerry Lettvin story here, but I can't recall the
right one).

